# Surviving mutants each package may carry, enforced by
# internal/tools/mutate in the scheduled Mutation workflow.
#
# A mutant is one small change to the code — a comparison swapped, a
# negation dropped, a 0 turned into a 1 — that the package's own tests
# did not notice. Each survivor is either a missing assertion or a change
# with no observable effect, and the ceiling exists because the second
# kind cannot be driven to zero: an error check that only decides whether
# a debug line is logged, a comparison the enclosing condition already
# settled, a guard the next line repeats. The commonest shape of all —
# the zero Go returns beside an error — is not mutated at all, so it never
# reaches this ceiling.
#
# Where a ceiling sits, decided 2026-09-28: at the measurement, with no
# slack. That is deliberately not how .coverage-floor works, and the
# difference is in what the two files count rather than in taste. A floor
# there sits just under what the area measures, because coverage is a
# percentage over thousands of statements and a refactor that improves the
# code moves it for reasons that are not a missing test; the slack absorbs
# that noise. A ceiling here counts named things — one mutant, at one line,
# with its reason written beside the number — so there is nothing for slack
# to absorb. The one source of noise that did exist, a mutant answering
# differently between runs, now fails on its own terms instead of hiding
# inside the number.
#
# Lowering a ceiling in a pull request that asserts more is welcome.
# Raising one is legitimate for a mutant shown to be unobservable, with the
# reason written here beside the number, the way every entry below does it.
# Raising one to absorb a survivor nobody has looked at is not: that
# survivor is a missing assertion, and this number is the only thing that
# will say so.
#
# The cost of no slack is latency, not noise, and it is worth naming. This
# ceiling stood at 15 for internal/adapter and measured exactly 15; #354
# then added a branch nothing asserted and took it to 16, four days later,
# with nothing wrong in the feature itself. The gate said so a week after
# the merge rather than in the pull request. Slack would not have fixed
# that — it would have hidden the missing assertion permanently rather than
# for a week, and the assertion was that a probe goes out at the protocol
# floor, which is not a detail. Whether this gate should also run per pull
# request for the packages a change touches is a separate question, and it
# is in ROADMAP.md rather than settled here.
#
# A survivor is re-run three more times before it is believed, and a mutant
# that is refused on one of those runs fails the workflow on its own terms —
# not as an over-budget verdict. A number that depends on which run produced
# it is a sample rather than a measurement, and the test that refuses the
# mutant only sometimes is flaky with respect to it: the fix is that test,
# never a number here. Two of these cost a day of hand measurement in the
# week of 2026-09-27, so the tool now names them.
#
# Every package listed here is run in full, so this file is also the
# scope: a package joins the run by being declared, with a measured
# ceiling attached. The pull-request half of the workflow reads that list
# from here rather than carrying a copy, and measures the entries whose
# directory the branch touched — so a package added here is measured on a
# pull request from the moment it is added, with nothing else to remember.
# internal/evidence measured 2026-09-29 at 253 mutants, 246 caught. This is
# the first declared package with nothing assertable left in it: every one
# of the five survivors is a change no input can distinguish, not a change
# nobody has got round to. Four are provably equivalent — a comparison the
# enclosing condition already settled, a file mode the kernel ignores
# without O_CREATE, and two truncation guards whose branch does what the
# branch below would have done — and the fifth has no input the timestamp
# layout admits. The number below is an irreducible remainder rather than a
# budget for unfinished work, which is what every ceiling here is meant to
# become.
#
# One of them used to flip. The loop bound in lessUTF16 was asserted only
# through Canonicalize, where the sort picks the comparison's argument order
# and a map's iteration order picks the permutation: mutated, the package
# caught it 21 times in 25 runs, and the scheduled workflow reported it as a
# survivor where a local run of the same sweep had not. A ceiling is not a
# measurement while one of its mutants answers differently one run in six.
#
# Both decisions this list used to carry are settled, and neither went the
# way the question was framed. Each was recorded as needing a seam in
# production code or permanent acceptance, and each turned out to need
# neither — but for different reasons, so the pair is worth keeping written
# down.
#
# The directory-sync tolerance was a condition, not a filesystem: naming it
# durabilityUnknown made it reachable with nothing added to the production
# path. The key write really was about making a write fail, and the way in
# was that the failure need not come from the filesystem — RLIMIT_FSIZE at
# zero refuses every write to a regular file while open, sync and close all
# still succeed. Both were called unreachable after looking at the
# filesystem and stopping there.
internal/evidence 5
# internal/adapter measured 2026-09-27 at 115 mutants, 105 caught. The ten
# survivors are four shapes: seven error checks that decide nothing but
# whether a debug line is logged; a channel buffer where both paths receive
# exactly once; a `> 0` widened to `>= 0`, where the branch it then takes
# does what the branch it skipped would have done; and a constant whose
# mutated form is the same number once the integer division truncates.
#
# It was 15 before, and the scheduled run of 2026-09-21 met that exactly:
# fifteen survivors against fifteen allowed, no margin at all. Protocol
# negotiation (#354, four days later) added the branch that sends the probe
# at the floor, nothing asserted the version on the wire, and the count
# went to 16 — so a ceiling with no room left was spent by the next feature
# that touched the package, which is the argument for lowering one as far
# as the measurement allows rather than to the first number that passes.
#
# The number also moved, which is worse than a number that is too high.
# Two copies of one verdict sat in branches a race chose between, so the
# same tree reported 16 survivors or 17 depending on which copy the tests
# reached — and 2026-09-21's run caught in CI what this machine did not.
# wait() keeps one copy of the sentence now.
internal/adapter  10
spec/evidence      1
