#
# The fourth is equivalent by measurement rather than by construction, and
# says so here because that is a weaker claim than the rest of this file
# makes. Reading the parse error and the sign the wrong way round matters
# only for a quantity too large for a float64, which ParseFloat returns as
# an infinity — and Go leaves the conversion of an out-of-range float to an
# int64 implementation-defined. On the platforms this builds for it lands
# on the most negative int64, which the next guard refuses, so the two
# answers agree. A port to a platform that saturates upward instead would
# make this mutant observable, and it would then be a missing assertion.
